8)How do you manage Terraform provider versioning?
I manage Terraform provider versioning by pinning provider versions in the configuration using version constraints, 
committing the terraform.lock.hcl file, and enforcing Terraform CLI version using required_version.
This ensures consistency across developers and CI/CD. 
Upgrades are done in a controlled manner using terraform init -upgrade and tested in lower environments before moving to production."

9)How would you provision infra across 10 AWS regions simultaneously?
First, I define the infrastructure using Infrastructure as Code, ideally Terraform with reusable modules for networking, compute, and security. 
I separate global resources like IAM, Route53, and S3 from regional components like VPCs, subnets, EKS/ECS, or RDS to avoid conflicts.
To deploy in parallel, I use either Terraform with Terragrunt or AWS CloudFormation StackSets. 
With Terraform, I create region-specific workspaces or state files and trigger parallel runs through a CI/CD pipeline using GitHub Actions or Tekton —
typically using a matrix job so each region is deployed at the same time.
Remote state is stored in S3 with DynamoDB for state locking.
Before rolling out to all 10 regions, I perform a canary deployment in one or two regions, run smoke tests, and then continue to the remaining regions
to ensure security and compliance across regions. Post-deployment, I verify using CloudWatch, AWS Config, and drift detection.

10)What to do when your Terraform state file becomes too large?
When a Terraform state file becomes too large, the best approach is to split the infrastructure into smaller, modular state files
to reduce blast radius and improve performance. 
I start by refactoring the configuration into logical modules such as networking, compute, databases, and security.
Each module gets its own state file by using separate Terraform workspaces, folders, or Terragrunt, and separate backend configurations.
Another strategy is enabling state compression and versioning—remote backends compress older state snapshots, and old versions can be pruned if not required. 
If many data sources or outputs are increasing state size, I reduce unnecessary outputs and avoid storing large data in the state.

